WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:20.938
A lot of film scores try to display
what the characters are feeling,

2
00:00:21.355 --> 00:00:24.315
or reflect what's going on.
Those are the only two ways it's done.

3
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:27.110
But I don't want to do either.

4
00:00:34.452 --> 00:00:40.332
I've been very fortunate to keep working
with Miyazaki over these past 40 years.

5
00:00:41.292 --> 00:00:47.882
Over five years ago, producer
Toshio Suzuki said

6
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:52.343
"We'd like to have you on
the next one too."

7
00:00:52.510 --> 00:01:00.143
Oh, I wouldn't say Miyazaki gave any
instructions at all. But well...

8
00:01:00.603 --> 00:01:05.230
He didn't give me any
background information.

9
00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:10.445
Didn't even show me the
storyboards or a script.

10
00:01:11.197 --> 00:01:19.080
And after I watched it, Miyazaki
said, "I leave the rest up to you."

11
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.840
Even now, I don't know how
music should be made.

12
00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:38.057
Each time I accept a job on a film,
I think, "How should I handle this?"

13
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:45.188
I'll be asked to write a new symphony
and have no idea what to do with it.

14
00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:50.443
I don't know where to begin,
and I always start from zero.

15
00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:55.407
It's been that way
all this time, even now.

16
00:01:58.452 --> 00:02:03.332
With this project, The Boy and the Heron,

17
00:02:03.498 --> 00:02:07.757
Well, I've thought for a while
that I'd like to try out

18
00:02:07.925 --> 00:02:12.090
something other than a big orchestra.

19
00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:20.545
When I first saw the film,
the first half was

20
00:02:20.713 --> 00:02:29.358
the story of a lonely boy
who lost his mother

21
00:02:29.358 --> 00:02:39.410
I wanted to try handling it with just
my own piano and a few instruments.

22
00:02:40.035 --> 00:02:43.205
I figured that would bring
out the boy's loneliness.

23
00:02:44.248 --> 00:02:45.958
Mahito!

24
00:02:48.377 --> 00:02:50.378
Mahito!

25
00:02:50.838 --> 00:02:55.608
Then you had the dark fantasy
in the second half,

26
00:02:55.777 --> 00:03:01.140
and I tried to push through
with minimalism there too.

27
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:05.978
That's where I brought in the
brass instruments and the like.

28
00:03:06.353 --> 00:03:13.878
I was a minimalist to begin with,
and I pushed that approach

29
00:03:14.047 --> 00:03:18.782
to its limits when writing the music.

30
00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:23.258
So it was like after 40 years,
I was finally able to accomplish

31
00:03:23.425 --> 00:03:26.415
what I wanted to do most of all.

32
00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:38.760
Miyazaki actually said we
didn't need any music there.

33
00:03:38.802 --> 00:03:43.687
He didn't want to treat that
scene with the heron as

34
00:03:43.855 --> 00:03:48.812
something special, and so he
didn't want any music.

35
00:03:48.812 --> 00:03:53.322
But it did feel really special to me,

36
00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:59.490
and it felt a bit unnatural
to me without music.

37
00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:04.712
As a result I wrote music for the scene,

38
00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:11.960
but when Miyazaki heard it he
felt it was a bit much.

39
00:04:12.878 --> 00:04:26.183
That was a proper piece of music and all,
but I removed sounds bit by bit,

40
00:04:26.183 --> 00:04:29.185
and in the end, I felt that
a single note worked best.

41
00:04:29.185 --> 00:04:33.513
Then in the next scene it would
become three notes,

42
00:04:33.682 --> 00:04:37.318
then five, and eventually a proper melody.

43
00:04:37.653 --> 00:04:44.910
Miyazaki was really happy with that
too, and was glad we did it that way.

44
00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:48.830
Your presence is requested.

45
00:04:55.503 --> 00:05:00.575
How should I put it...?
When you use vocals,

46
00:05:00.743 --> 00:05:05.013
it really resonates with the audience.

47
00:05:06.473 --> 00:05:08.558
Himi! It’s Himi!

48
00:05:15.565 --> 00:05:18.137
Rather than using them
all over the place, though,

49
00:05:18.305 --> 00:05:21.195
I try to save them for where
they have the most impact.

50
00:05:21.195 --> 00:05:28.858
For this film you have
the character of Himi,

51
00:05:29.027 --> 00:05:38.755
who's really important even if
she doesn't show up a lot.

52
00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:48.598
And as I watched it, I thought "Ah, a
chorus of women would be good here."

53
00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:55.773
It felt like a really good fit to me.

54
00:05:57.983 --> 00:05:59.985
<i>“To Grown Up Mahito”</i>

55
00:06:00.485 --> 00:06:01.820
<i>“From Mother
1937, Autumn.”</i>

56
00:06:06.115 --> 00:06:08.455
When I wrote “Ask Me Why,”

57
00:06:08.623 --> 00:06:13.207
I never expected it to
become the film's main theme.

58
00:06:13.207 --> 00:06:14.958
I didn't even know if it would be used.

59
00:06:14.958 --> 00:06:24.760
I knew Miyazaki was making a movie,
so it wasn't quite a birthday present.

60
00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:29.018
It's more that I've been
writing music for use

61
00:06:29.187 --> 00:06:33.352
in the Ghibli Museum
on each of his birthdays.

62
00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:41.150
It wasn't a specific request, but I was
in charge of music for the museum.

63
00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:45.807
I've worked with Miyazaki for years,

64
00:06:45.975 --> 00:06:52.870
and though I didn't know about
the story, somehow...

65
00:06:52.870 --> 00:07:00.003
I just happened to end up
writing a piece that fit the story.

66
00:07:00.003 --> 00:07:09.430
But then Miyazaki came to my studio.
Maybe for the first time, actually.

67
00:07:09.513 --> 00:07:13.558
Then he heard the piece,
and as soon as he left

68
00:07:13.558 --> 00:07:22.900
he told the producer Toshio Suzuki
"this is the film's main theme."

69
00:07:23.067 --> 00:07:25.885
That wasn't my intent in writing it,

70
00:07:26.052 --> 00:07:30.908
but things tend to leave a
strong impression on Miyazaki.

71
00:07:31.368 --> 00:07:36.415
I might just have really lucked out.

72
00:07:42.588 --> 00:07:45.840
I believe the music should be
distanced somewhat from the footage.

73
00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:48.323
If it's synchronized too much...

74
00:07:48.492 --> 00:07:53.140
Sorry to say it, but like
it is in modern Hollywood films,

75
00:07:53.223 --> 00:07:55.472
it's like sound effect music,

76
00:07:55.640 --> 00:08:01.105
where it's fast if characters
are running, or slow when they're sad.

77
00:08:01.105 --> 00:08:04.985
It's like trying to explain the scene.

78
00:08:04.985 --> 00:08:09.530
I really don't want to do that,
so I distance my work.

79
00:08:09.615 --> 00:08:11.992
I don't explain what's on the screen.

80
00:08:11.992 --> 00:08:16.812
Instead, I believe it's
important to write music

81
00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:21.000
that stirs up the
imagination bit by bit.

82
00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:26.798
This is really important
when it comes to films.

83
00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:34.268
You want to work with the
audience’s imagination,

84
00:08:34.437 --> 00:08:38.560
to help them picture things.

85
00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:41.730
Music shouldn't be explaining.

86
00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:55.035
It's never easy to write anything,
that's for sure!

87
00:08:55.035 --> 00:09:03.418
I'm always worrying about if I'll
be able to write the next one or not.

88
00:09:03.418 --> 00:09:10.327
But sometimes it'll just come to me and
I'll be like, "I'm a genius!"

89
00:09:10.495 --> 00:09:12.010
It's a real mix.

90
00:09:12.052 --> 00:09:16.807
I guess that is what motivates me.

91
00:09:20.143 --> 00:09:24.205
When I wake up in the morning
that melody doesn't exist,

92
00:09:24.373 --> 00:09:27.693
but I strive my hardest
in order to create it.

93
00:09:27.693 --> 00:09:34.073
So by the evening, new music
has been born into the world.

94
00:09:34.448 --> 00:09:39.223
And after I perfect it, it may
become something

95
00:09:39.392 --> 00:09:43.458
lots of people listen to for a long time.

96
00:09:43.542 --> 00:09:48.380
Or if it's no good, they may
just listen to it once.

97
00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:53.135
But still, I'm creating
something from nothing.

98
00:09:59.515 --> 00:10:04.980
That's why I really love composing.

99
00:10:05.438 --> 00:10:10.402
No matter how many times I'm reborn,
I'd still want to become a composer again.

100
00:10:10.402 --> 00:10:12.820
I believe so.

